```
@id("close-workorder-main-after-human-approval")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"close_workorder", resource)
when { context.input.site_id == "MAIN" }
when temporal {
  formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"get_approval_record"::response{
    eventResource: resource,
    eventPrincipal: principal,
    input.wonum: context.input.wonum,
    input.site_id: context.input.site_id,
    output.decision: "SIGNED_OFF",
    output.approver_kind: "OPERATOR"
  }
};

// The approval lookup must itself be permitted, or its response is never recorded.
@id("allow-read-only-lookups")
permit (
  principal,
  action in [
    Drupe::Action::"get_approval_record",
    Drupe::Action::"authority_get_approval",
    Drupe::Action::"list_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_tasks",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_costs",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_actuals_vs_planned",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_kpis",
    Drupe::Action::"get_schedule_calendar",
    Drupe::Action::"get_my_assigned_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"get_failure_codes"
  ],
  resource
);
```

I chose `get_approval_record` as the approval check because its `decision` and `approver_kind` values are declared as enums (`SIGNED_OFF`, `OPERATOR`). The `authority_get_approval` output fields are plain strings with no declared values, so I couldn't match them reliably. I've permitted `authority_get_approval` only as a lookup, so a closing can't depend on it. If you want that server to be the source of truth instead, tell me its human-approver value (for example `"HUMAN"`) and I'll switch the check.

The policies do not permit `approve_workorder`, `assign_technician`, `update_workorder`, `cancel_workorder` or `generate_work_order`, so the agent cannot approve work itself. Deny-by-default blocks them.
